WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.410
If you enjoy random short facts about entertaining
events in history you most certainly came

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:07.479
across this famous “did you know” post.

00:00:07.479 --> 00:00:13.080
Which says that during the 1866 Austro-Prussian
War Liechtenstein sided with Austria and went

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:15.959
to war with its military which was 80 men
strong.

00:00:15.959 --> 00:00:21.440
These 80 soldiers saw no action during this
conflict and after it ended the 80 men returned

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:26.550
home with an extra 81st person who was said
to be an Italian friend the soldiers made

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:27.880
during the war.

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:32.110
Hence, Liechtenstein's army arrived home stronger
than it departed.

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:38.719
This was also the last war Lichtenstein participated
in as it disbanded its army in 1868 and announced

00:00:38.719 --> 00:00:40.910
permanent neutrality then on.

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:42.670
Now, is this story true?

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:46.489
Well if you have seen any of my other “well
actually videos” you should know by now

00:00:46.489 --> 00:00:51.160
that oftentimes these things are based in
truth but not entirely true.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:53.269
So let’s see where this one goes wrong.

00:00:53.269 --> 00:00:58.789
In the latter half of the 19th century Lichtenstein
was ruled by Johanne II Prince of Liechtenstein,

00:00:58.789 --> 00:01:04.710
he was a fairly liberal ruler for his time
and in 1862 even issued Lichtenstein’s first

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:05.750
constitution.

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:10.160
However also during this time he along with
the entire Lichtenstein’s upper class had

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:15.960
very close ties to the Austria Empire meaning
when the Austro-Prussian war started in 1866,

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:22.110
the Lichtenstein upper class was, let's say
very strongly persuaded to join Austria.

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:26.470
This didn’t sit all too well with the Liechtenstein
people who with their new constitutional government

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:31.110
heavily voiced their concerns, like, costs
of going to war, the fact that they didn’t

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:35.090
want to fight against fellow Germans, and
simply that no Liechtensteiner really felt

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:38.590
like dying in a war would be a pleasant experience.

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:42.730
So the Prince being pressured by Austria on
one side and by his subjects on the other

00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:45.810
had to do some clever political maneuvering.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:50.030
First he assured the government that he will
take on all the costs of the military campaign

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:54.500
(except the mobilization), second, he told
his subjects that no Lichtensteiner will have

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:59.381
to fight against fellow Germans as the Liechtenstein
army would be sent against the Italians (who

00:01:59.381 --> 00:02:03.630
were supporting the Prussians in the war),
and third, the military was technically under

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:08.210
the direct control of the Prince so in the
end under the premise of defending Liechtenstein

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:12.709
against a plausible invading Italian army
he went around the Lichtenstein’s government

00:02:12.709 --> 00:02:16.620
and sent the Liechtenstein military to guard
the Tyrol mountain passes with the Austrian

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:18.420
Tyrol infantry regiments.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:22.930
This way Lichtenstein was practically helping
out Austria, making them happy, but at the

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:27.290
same time as stated before the premise of
Liechtenstein's military action was officially

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:31.930
defencive, meaning they technically never
declared any war, making the Lichtensteainers

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:32.930
happy.

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:34.260
(At Least somewhat)

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:37.629
This combined with the fact that over the
course of the 6 weeks from the Lichtenstein’s

00:02:37.629 --> 00:02:41.629
army departure in July till the end of the
war in September the Lichetnstein army never

00:02:41.629 --> 00:02:46.749
actively fought anyone, means that as you
can see on this map Lichtenstein is officially

00:02:46.749 --> 00:02:49.069
considered as being neutral during the war.

00:02:49.069 --> 00:02:54.030
It was never even mentioned in the Peace Treaty
because despite their support of Austria,

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:57.650
Lichtenstein was never considered by Prussia
as being at war with them.

00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:05.890
So was the 1866 Austro-Prussian war the last
war Lichetnstein was in, technically no, practically

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:06.890
I guess?

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:11.370
Ok, ok but what of the soldiers, where there
truly just 80 soldiers that guarded the Tyrol

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:12.370
Pass?

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:16.489
Well the Lichetnstein military at the time
had 82 men and 18 in reserve.

00:03:16.489 --> 00:03:20.810
However according to this one source 2 men
stayed behind to guard the barracks in Vaduz

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:21.810
castle.

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:25.459
Plus considering that the reserves most likely
wouldn’t go to Tyrol means that actually

00:03:25.459 --> 00:03:27.719
80 men participated in the war.

00:03:27.719 --> 00:03:30.250
So that part is correct.

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:34.709
Lastly did the Lichetnstein soldiers actually
come back with an Italian friend?

00:03:34.709 --> 00:03:40.139
Well considering they went to Tyrol to defend
against the Italians it would be very unlikely,

00:03:40.139 --> 00:03:42.019
and in fact it didn’t happen.

00:03:42.019 --> 00:03:46.269
However that’s not to say the soldiers didn’t
come back with an extra person, they did.

00:03:46.269 --> 00:03:51.230
A lieutenant of the Kaiserjäger, an Austrian
Tyrol infantry regiment, accompanied the Liechtenstein

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:53.859
soldiers home as a military liaison.

00:03:53.859 --> 00:03:57.999
Which would make sense because as mentioned
before the Liechtenstein's army cooperated

00:03:57.999 --> 00:04:00.610
with the Tyrol infantry regiment during the
war.

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:05.069
Whether this Austrian lieutenant was their
friend, I would like to think so but he most

00:04:05.069 --> 00:04:08.950
likely returned back home to Austria after
all was said and done.

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:13.329
Also the state administrator (Carl Haus von
Hausen) and the advisor to the state and the

00:04:13.329 --> 00:04:18.310
Prince Clemens Graf von Westphalen met up
with the army on the Liechtenstein border

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:24.410
and entered Lichtenstein with them, so technically
Lichtenstein army returned with 3 extra men.

00:04:24.410 --> 00:04:29.460
However these 2 were Liechtensteiners or atleast
people who worked for Liechtenstein just doing

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:31.770
their job so I wouldn’t really count it.

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:36.550
In the end Liechtenstein participated in the
Austro Prussian war, kind of.

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:39.110
It had a military of 80 men, kind of.

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:42.160
It returned with an extra friend, kind of.

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:48.180
And 2 years after in 1868 it disbanded its
army and declared permanent neutrality henceforth,

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:52.340
so the Austro Prussian war was the last Lichtenstein
war, kind of.

